Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "offensive reorganization after player absence",
    "change in points per possession",
    "baseline L (league average or team-with-player baseline)",
    "predictive relationship"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to measure offensive reorganization (relative to L) from play-by-play and graph-derived play types?",
    "How to isolate change in points per possession during absence spells, accounting for opponent and game context?",
    "How to achieve sufficient power given small number of absence spells and high variance in PPP?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF20",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "V1 (structure moves under real absence) confirmed: 21/23 absences above null median, V1(a) 16/23 above own-null p95, V1(b) mean(D_real - median_null)=+0.142, CI[+0.103,+0.187]. V2 (LOO simulation predicts the magnitude) failed: Spearman rho=+0.288, p=0.077, CI[-0.244,+0.667]; V2b rho=+0.253, p=0.115 NOT confirmed."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-009",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Comparison of player-routed PPP between V4 and V5: 8 divergences, 0 scoreable because players were missing from Neo4j graph; V5 mostly routed to 'team'. Hit rate not recorded."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF31",
      "status_as_recorded": "artifact",
      "finding": "Apparent PPP edge from low-n cells (e.g., +1.26 PPP at n>=5) is small-sample inflation driven by role players with 2-3 PPP on 5-7 possessions. The n>=15 gate filters noise and recommended."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF04",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Early suppression triggers may yield better BEST outcomes (early BEST-AVOID gap 0.701 vs late 0.415), but evidence weak (p=0.031, n=57) and not robust across all series."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-009",
      "what_failed": "direct comparison of player-routed PPP between routing versions",
      "why": "missing players in graph database; routing defaulted to 'team', making player-level PPP scoring impossible (0/8 scoreable)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF31",
      "what_failed": "using low-n cells to estimate player-routing PPP advantage",
      "why": "small-sample inflation produced artifactually high PPP (e.g., 3.00 on 5-7 possessions); the apparent edge disappeared under the n>=15 gate."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF20",
      "what_failed": "magnitude prediction of structure change via leave-one-out simulation",
      "why": "correlation not significant (rho=0.288, p=0.077), confidence interval included zero, likely due to small sample (23 absences) and high variance."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "SF20 shows that offensive association structure changes detectably during player absences, but predicting the magnitude of that change from a simulation was unsuccessful with only 23 absences.",
    "SF31 demonstrates that small-sample PPP metrics are highly susceptible to inflation and artifacts, necessitating minimum possession thresholds (n>=15).",
    "SF-APPS-009 reveals that player-level PPP routing from the graph database can suffer from missing player data, potentially biasing reorganization metrics that rely on initiator identification."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No prior study directly tested whether a metric of offensive reorganization predicts change in points per possession.",
    "The predictive power of any particular reorganization metric (e.g., play-type distribution shift) remains unknown.",
    "The sample size required for adequate power to detect a plausible effect size (if it exists) is unclear."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The proposed study would need to define reorganization relative to a baseline L (e.g., team-with-player play-type distribution) and compute PPP change during the absence spell.",
    "Given SF20's failure to predict magnitude with 23 spells, 148 spells may still be underpowered for a small effect, but could be sufficient if the effect is moderate.",
    "Confounding by replacement quality, opponent strength, and game script will be severe and must be controlled for.",
    "The graph-derived play-type data may have similar missing-data issues as player routing, affecting reorganization measurement."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The exact operationalization of 'offense reorganizes (relative to L)' is not specified; L could be league average or team-with-player baseline, each with different implications.",
    "148 absence spells may include very short spells (1-2 games) where PPP change is highly noisy and reorganization is not meaningful.",
    "The distribution of spell lengths and the number of possessions per spell is unknown, affecting the reliability of PPP change estimates."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "The history (SF20 V2 failure) indicates that predicting a magnitude change from a reorganization metric is difficult with small samples. SF31 highlights artifact risk from low-n cells, which is directly relevant to any PPP-based analysis of absence spells. SF-APPS-009 warns about missing data in the graph database. Consequently, the decision shifts toward requiring a larger pooled sample, per-spell minimum possession thresholds, and explicit robustness checks before proceeding."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.55,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "With 148 spells, a true correlation of 0.2-0.3 may be detectable only if noise is low; prior work suggests smaller effect sizes are likely."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Reorganization metrics derived from play-type graphs may suffer from missing player data and routing errors, reducing signal."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Unobserved factors like replacement player skill, game context, and tactical adjustments could drive both reorganization and PPP change."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": ["FAM:SF20", "FAM:SF31", "FAM:SF-APPS-009"],
    "summary": "SF20 confirmed structure changes during absences but could not predict magnitude. SF31 warns against small-sample PPP artifacts. SF-APPS-009 shows data gaps in player-level PPP routing."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "148 absence spells may not yield sufficient power if the true effect size is small; prior attempt with 23 spells was underpowered (rho 0.288, p=0.077)."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Reorganization (relative to L) must be measured from play-type possessions; routing errors and missing player data (as in SF-APPS-009) could introduce noise."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Change in PPP is confounded by opponent defensive quality, rest days, replacement player ability, and game script; omitted variable bias likely without careful control."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "If spells with extreme reorganization are selected, regression to the mean in PPP change could spuriously inflate or deflate correlation."
    },
    {
      "code": "LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD",
      "text": "Defining baseline L using data from after the absence period would leak future information into the predictor."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "If multiple reorganization metrics or different spell-length thresholds are explored without pre-registration, false-positive risk increases."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.15,
    "rejected": 0.20,
    "untestable": 0.40,
    "artifact": 0.15,
    "uncertain": 0.10
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The rank correlation between a pre-registered reorganization metric (the cosine distance between the team's play-type distribution during the absence spell and its baseline with-player distribution, relative to league-wide changes) and the change in team points per possession (PPP) during the same spell, controlling for confounders.",
    "unit": "An absence spell: a continuous block of regular-season games missed by a single offensive core player (USG% > 20% in the prior 20 games) due to injury, rest, or personal reasons, excluding suspensions.",
    "sample": "All qualifying absence spells from NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 regular seasons, with minimum spell length of 5 games and at least 15 offensive possessions per game in the spell; the with-player baseline is the 20 games immediately preceding the spell. Preliminary estimate ~120 spells.",
    "power": "Assuming a true Spearman rho of 0.25, two-tailed alpha 0.05, n=120 yields power >0.80; if the effect is smaller (rho=0.15), power drops to ~0.35, so a sensitivity analysis using Bayesian methods is planned.",
    "controls": "Primary analysis is rank correlation with no controls; secondary partial Spearman correlation controlling for (1) opponent defensive rating during spell, (2) team offensive rating in the baseline period, (3) home/away game proportion, (4) days since last game, (5) spell length in games, (6) season fixed effects.",
    "falsification": "1. Randomly reassign absence spells to different players' spells and recompute reorganization-PPP correlations; the true distribution should not differ from the null. 2. Test on a hold-out season (2025-26) if sample permits, comparing observed correlation to the training result.",
    "multiplicity": "Primary hypothesis: reorganization metric vs PPP change. Secondary explorations (e.g., by play-type category) are labeled as exploratory and corrected with Holm-Bonferroni. Only the primary test will be used for inference.",
    "decision_rule": "Reject null if the primary Spearman rho has p < 0.05 (two-tailed) and a bootstrap 95% confidence interval does not cross zero. If rejected, check robustness: the effect must remain significant in the partial correlation and in the hold-out validation (if available) at p<0.10.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 to 2025-26 regular-season play-by-play and derived possession graph, as frozen for the 2025-26 season; all code and data versions committed to a repository before analysis."
  }
}

